Gradient-based hyperparameter optimization has gained widespread popularity in the domain of few-shot meta-learning. However, it remains largely impractical for tasks involving long horizons—many gradient steps—due to challenges related to memory consumption and gradient degradation. A common approach is to learn hyperparameters online, but this method tends to be greedy, resulting in substantial performance decline. 

In response, we introduce Forward-Mode Differentiation with Sharing (FDS), a straightforward and efficient algorithm that addresses memory scalability through forward-mode differentiation and mitigates gradient degradation by sharing hyperparameters across contiguous time steps. We offer theoretical guarantees concerning the noise reduction capabilities of our method and demonstrate its empirical effectiveness by differentiating through approximately 10,000 unrolled gradient steps during optimization. 

Our experiments on large hyperparameter search spaces with CIFAR-10 show that FDS significantly outperforms greedy gradient-based methods, while also achieving a 20-fold speedup compared to current black-box approaches. The implementation code is available at: \url{https://github.com/polo5/FDS}
